<p>Alright, I have to ask&#8230;</p>
<p>So how am I reading about it then?</p>
<p>The 'requires an in-person visit' doesn't hold up against the anomalous effect of preventing information regarding the skip in any format from leaving the town. Unless I personally went to the town to read this, I shouldn't be able to have this information, right?</p>
<p>I think you built too perfect of a prison here. I'm going to re-read to see if I missed something, but simply because it's (mostly) technically sound and well written I will be no voting for now.</p>
<p>Edit: Upon rereading I see that I forgot the note that some very general information is able to be communicated, but I think that explanation is frankly a little too convenient. The information in this article isn't general at all, there's a lot of very specific info (pretty much any info you'd need to write a skip) so that by the end it didn't even seem like there were any antimemetic properties whatsoever.</p>
<p>I think a LOT of this info would need to be removed for them to be convincingly anomalous, because right now I know everything I would need to know about these kids, as far as I can tell.</p>

